From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Wed, 12 Oct 1994 09:14:34 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-10-198] Challenge to uploaders

Nancy Lebovitz writes:

> The syllogism is fallacious, but so is a syllogism which concludes
> that consciousness does *not* require particular physical properties

Yes, that's correct, and thanks for the elaboration.  Robin's
answer to Ed was better than mine -- we don't know for sure
that 'consciousness' will arise in computer programs or anything
else other than brains.

However, if a definition of consciousness on which we can agree (hah)
does not have requirements that can only be satisfied by brains,
then those requirements can be met in other ways, perhaps by
general-purpose computers (because of their general modeling power)
or perhaps by other constructed devices.

Given that at present we cannot prove it one way or another, we
must look at what we think is most likely.  I personally have
a hard time imagining a satisfactory definition of consciousness
that resorts to specific unmodelable neurophysiological features.
Therefore I lean toward the possibility of 'consciousness' occurring
in properly constructed/programmed computers.  But of course I have
no proof and could be convinced otherwise by a persuasive argument.
Penrose seems to be trying to build such an argument.  I do not
find it convincing at present.  Searle also has tried to argue
the case, but his argument isn't even close to being convincing.

Unfortunately, we would need a rather precise definition of
'consciousness' to even make a determination.  My guess is
that no such satisfactory definition will ever appear.  We
seem to use the word 'consciousness' to refer to a vaguely-defined
set of internal processes and feelings; how can we have a clear
objective test for something if we can't even define it?

For this reason, practical matters are likely to be of more import,
as others in this thread have already pointed out, in various ways.

derek


