From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Oct 94 10:23:53 EST
Subject: [#94-10-199] Challenge to uploaders/Mind as Book

But to continue with the book analogy, what if we record all information about 
me in a gigantic book with billions of pages.  Is this me?  I don't think so.  

All the information defining me is there.  There may even be flowcharts that can 
predict my behavior:  Ask any question you want, and the flowcharts lead you to 
the appropriate answer that sound like something I would say and also insist 
that "this book you are reading is alive!"  I would not be happy with this 
definition of "me".  If someone published such a book about me after I had died, 
I would not consider this an acceptable form of "resurrection"

Even if you replace the "reader" with a computer that will output the text with 
a voice synthesizer that sounds like my voice and inputs your questions with a 
voice recognition program, I still am not sure that this would be anything more 
than a fancy book or videotape of me.  It looks like me (like a picture), it 
moves like me (like a video), it talks like me (like a recording), it is 
interactive like me (like some of my software programs I write today), but none 
of these things have created a duplicate me yet.

Even a great recording/duplication of me does not alter my current consciousness 
in my own meat-brain.  And when this meat-brain goes to sleep, I experience 
sleep, not what the other "copies" of me experience.  And when this meat-brain 
shuts down for good, I won't experience anything.  Even if new me's are created 
that are actually alive, *this* me still doesn't want to die.  Creating new me's 
is just about as good as having children to continue your genetic memes (which I 
have no intention of doing).  It just doesn't feel like immortality to this me.
__
