From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Wed, 12 Oct 1994 18:27:24 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-204] Vinge talk

Apologies for my tardy replies.  Netcom ate my connection for a
few days.

Years ago Langdon Winner wrote a poor excuse for a book called
_Autonomous Technology_ in which he described *contemporary*
technology in just the sort of terms that Vinge applies to the
machines that are supposed to lie on the other side of the
sigularity.  And of course one can imagine a Victorian Vinge,
rising to address an audience in 1894.  Over the next century, he
might say, our technology is going to become unimaginably
complex.  Nobody will understand how anything works. Machines
will deliver force in ways that are not only faster, more
powerful, and more accurate than humans; they will be faster and
more accurate than we can track with our senses.  We are going to
need machines to govern our machines.  In every respect we will
become completely dependent on their functioning properly.

This Victorian would have been completely right, of course.  An
18th century Vinge might have made a forecast with much the same
tone about the 19th century (railroads!, steamships!, telegraphs
and the telephone!, steel!, the gatling gun!, and so on), and
here we have the real Vinge reading his 1994 script.  What's the
lesson?  The lesson is that it is always possible to describe
technology in ways that make it sound as though it had a mind of
its own -- especially when people try to imagine the machines of
the future -- but that those machines never look that way to
people actually using them at the time.  At least not to people
with an engineer's perspective.  Winner doesn't count.

