The most interesting subplot of _Blade Runner_ is about uploading, when you
think about it.  Only they present it as something else.  Tyrell's secretary,
Rachel, is a "replicant" who has been "given the memories of" Tyrell's niece.
This is explained as a "cushion" to soften some sort of existential stress
that normal memoryless replicants supposedly go through.

They don't say (for instance) that she's Tyrell's niece who has been
given the body of a replicant.  And neither she nor Deckerd (the
Harrison Ford character) think of it that way either.

But they have a point.  In the scene where Deckerd tells Rachel about her
replicanthood, he tells her some memories of hers and says, "Did you ever
tell that to anyone?"  It's a moving scene because she's finding out her
whole life is a lie.  Also she's suffering human grief, learning that
she's not quite human.  Which makes her a little more human than the
rest of humanity--but you already know that's what the whole story's
about; I want to mention a couple neet things to watch for the next time
you see the movie.

In that scene, she throws down a photograph and leaves.  Deckerd picks it
up.  It's "her" as a girl with her mother.  In the non-Directors Cut
version of the movie, he voice-overs something about what it would be
like to have someone else's memories.  Then, a split second before he puts
it down, the picture (which is filling the screen) moves.  It's almost
imperceptible--some shadows shift against the girl, her mother and the
porch they're sitting on.  They're shadows of a tree waving in a breeze,
but the effect is that for an instant you feel the breeze and see the
sunlight.  It's about my favorite half-second of any movie.  Because for
her it isn't a picture, it's something she lived...only she didn't.

I'm not sure it's in the same place in The Director's Cut (which is a much
better movie).  It is in there but maybe at a later point in the movie.

She could see herself as a niece who's been uploaded but she doesn't,
showing that certain "minor" details impinge on issues of identity, like:

   Nobody told Tyrell's niece she was going to be uploaded.
   Or, they erased that particular memory in Rachel.
   Nobody told Rachel she was a replicant or an upload.
   In the movie, replicants are made to be slaves, and killed if they
      return to Earth.  Also, they only live five or six years.  Not
      necessarily a step "up" from being human.
   Evidently some--but how much?--of the niece's memory became known to
      Tyrell and other people in the transfer process.
   The niece, whom Rachel (but that must be the niece's name too, right?)
      doesn't even know, knows everything about Rachel up to some unknown
      point--and how much has she revealed or will she reveal to others?

In a couple of real senses, Rachel's memories are neither true nor her own.

Another great scene is where Rachel plays the piano in Deckerd's apartment.
She says, "I remember...lessons...but I don't know whether that was me or
Deckerd's niece."  I think those are her very words.  Leaving out some
bits, Deckerd kisses her, and she makes to leave the apartment in a rush,
but Deckerd stops her.  This scene seems strange and violent unless you
pay attention to one line a friend pointed out to me.

Deckerd stops her at the door looking fierce, and says, "Kiss me."
She mumbles, (this is the line), "I can't rely on..."
He says, "Say, 'Kiss me.'"
She says, "Kiss me," and he does.
"Now you kiss me," and she does.
"Say, 'I want you.'"
"I want you."

The last time Deckerd is at his apartment he finds the door ajar and Rachel
inside asleep but undisturbed.  As they're leaving Deckerd notices an
origami unicorn on the floor by the door.  But (only in The Director's Cut,
I think) there's a scene earlier in the movie.  Deckerd's dreaming.  We're
in a green forest, much different from anything else in the movie.  Into a
clearing comes...a unicorn!  And Deckerd wakes up.  Now how did the guy
who made the origami know about the unicorn?  And besides, how come
Deckerd takes repeated poundings by these superhuman replicants and
keeps on going...?

 --Steve


